<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  environment_image</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&lt;image-at-sha256-digest&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  fixture_digest</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&lt;sha256&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  network_policy</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">model-proxy-only</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">resources</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  cpu_cores</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#B5CEA8">2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  memory_limit_mib</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#B5CEA8">4096</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">budget</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  agent_wall_seconds</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#B5CEA8">900</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  assistant_steps</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#B5CEA8">80</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  verifier_wall_seconds</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#B5CEA8">120</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">acceptance</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  verifier_revision</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&lt;full-commit-sha&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  required</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: [</span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">behavior</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">, </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">regression</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">, </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">integrity</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">  protected_paths</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: [</span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">tests/**</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">, </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">public-api.json</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">]</span></span>
</code></pre></div></div></div></div><div class="code-block-fade-overlay print:hidden" data-fade-overlay="true" aria-hidden="true" style="--fade-color-light:#ffffff;--fade-color-dark:#0B0C0E"></div></div>
<span data-as="p">This is an example eval-layer protocol, not mcode’s configuration format. The angle-bracket values get replaced with the real commit, image digest, and file digests when the task is checked in. The 900 seconds and 80 steps are also just example budgets that need calibrating per task.</span>
<span data-as="p">The agent can see the requirements, the code, and the public tests. The reference fix, hidden tests, and grading program stay on the eval side. Keeping them in the same repo for management is fine, but they must be mounted separately; otherwise the answer may get shipped in along with the question.</span>
<span data-as="p">Resource limits also have to actually take effect. A config that says 4 GiB but allows temporary overshoot can produce different results from one that kills the process the moment it hits the limit. Record the limits that were actually applied.</span>
<span data-as="p"><a href="https://docs.harborframework.com/core-concepts/tasks/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="link">Harbor’s task format</a> bundles instructions, environment, and acceptance scripts together and is a good reference. Before a task is checked in, I first confirm that the unfixed version really fails and that the reference fix passes reliably. I also rerun it in a clean environment to rule out leftover local files and external dependencies like dead web pages. Otherwise you run tests all night and discover the next morning that the task itself was broken.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="02-the-runner-does-more-than-launch-a-process"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#02-the-runner-does-more-than-launch-a-process" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">02 The Runner does more than launch a process</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">Some terminology first: a task is one problem; a trial is one attempt at that problem by a given version. Every trial gets its own runtime environment, data directory, and artifact directory. Memory, login configuration, caches, and background processes from the previous run must not quietly affect the next.</span>
<span data-as="p">The Runner executes in the stages below. State transitions are persisted to disk so that after a scheduler restart it knows which trials haven’t started, which are still running, and which only need grading. Running a task twice costs extra money and can mix artifacts from two runs into one result.</span>
<span data-ramka-trigger="" class="zoom-image-trigger contents cursor-zoom-in"><span class="contents"><picture class="contents"><img data-path="images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg" src="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=e99c35db3a86002f599d97ec3083a936" alt="One trial, a complete record: prepare, run the agent, freeze artifacts, verify independently, seal and clean up" class="object-contain" width="1536" height="1024" decoding="async" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" srcSet="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=280&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=48d8dc192f4ba37e929653a04cc197b8 280w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=560&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=c7da965092f1c147b2cac4a235b8ca11 560w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=840&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=3b0f48e993a087610173ba92ed2891c0 840w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=1100&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=d40c581221d142055d789c2070a73aed 1100w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=1650&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=2d6093ca35a2b736694a74ceb4415c34 1650w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-runner.jpg?w=2500&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=e829ec6ee7fecb511e7dc79eed47e9c0 2500w" data-optimize="true" style="aspect-ratio:1536 / 1024"/></picture></span></span><button type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-ramka-trigger="" aria-label="Expand image" class="inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-black/20 bg-white/55 text-neutral-900 shadow-[0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] backdrop-blur-[40px] backdrop-saturate-150 transition-[background-color,box-shadow,scale,opacity,color] duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-white/65 active:scale-[0.96] active:bg-white/75 focus:outline-hidden focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.25),0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-30 disabled:active:scale-100 dark:border-white/25 dark:bg-neutral-900/45 dark:text-white dark:shadow-[0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] dark:hover:bg-neutral-900/55 dark:active:bg-neutral-900/65 dark:focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(255_255_255/0.4),0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] absolute z-10 size-px overflow-hidden whitespace-nowrap [clip-path:inset(50%)] pointer-events-none focus-visible:size-9 focus-visible:overflow-visible focus-visible:[clip-path:none] focus-visible:pointer-events-auto in-[a]:hidden"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-4 shrink-0"><path d="M10.75 2.75H15.25V7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M15.25 2.75L10.75 7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 10.75V15.25H7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 15.25L7.25 10.75" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></button>
<span data-as="p"><em>The Runner manages run state; raw records are kept on the host side, and the agent cannot rewrite them.</em></span>
<span data-as="p">During preparation, verify the task fingerprint, image, and initial files, confirm the environment is healthy, then start the agent. When execution ends, first stop background processes and file writes, freeze the artifacts, then hand them to the grader. Even when something fails midway, collect evidence and clean up the environment.</span>
<span data-as="p">It’s easy to underestimate how far isolation has to go. Creating a Git worktree doesn’t clear memory stored in the user data directory, and it doesn’t isolate ports, plugin configuration, or external databases. External services used by each trial need their own namespaces, and resource quotas must be enforced. Read-only dependency caches can be shared, but their contents must be pinned; whether timing includes cache warm-up also needs to be agreed on.</span>
<span data-as="p">I keep two layers of timeouts: the agent’s own task time limit, plus a Runner watchdog. When internal cancellation fails, the outside can still kill the whole process group or container and record why it was force-terminated. Time environment setup, agent execution, and verification separately; otherwise a slightly slow dependency download can get counted as an agent performance regression.</span>
<span data-as="p">If the experiment has a cost cap, billing has to cover model calls from retries, compaction, and sub-tasks. A unified model gateway can accumulate usage and enforce a stop policy, with the usage in the final answer kept for reconciliation. If you only learn the cost after the answer arrives, it’s too late to control the budget. Pin the price table and cache pricing as well, and flag missing usage separately.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="03-run-tasks-with-the-real-minimax-code"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#03-run-tasks-with-the-real-minimax-code" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">03 Run tasks with the real MiniMax Code</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">The adapter only launches, reads output, stops, and collects artifacts. Tool scheduling, context compaction, and model retries stay with MiniMax Code. Rewriting an execution loop just to make evaluation convenient risks replacing exactly the Harness behavior you most want to test.</span>
<span data-as="p">MiniMax Code already has <code>mcode exec</code>, which can set the working directory, config, timeout, and step limit, and emit <code>stream-json</code>. Inside a prepared isolated environment, it’s launched like this. The Runner fills in the variables; the corresponding directories and config must be prepared beforehand.</span>
