" tabindex="-1" class="size-full rounded-[inherit] [--scroll-area-fade-size:32px] py-3.5 px-4 overflow-y-hidden! base-ui-disable-scrollbar" style="overflow:scroll" data-component-part="scroll-area-viewport"><div role="presentation" style="min-width:fit-content" data-component-part="scroll-area-content" class="min-w-full! h-full children:my-0! children:shadow-none! children:bg-transparent!"><div class="font-mono whitespace-pre leading-6"><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light-default dark-plus" style="background-color:#ffffff;--shiki-dark-bg:#0B0C0E;color:#1f2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4" language="json"><code language="json" numberOfLines="12"><span class="line"><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">{</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;trial_id&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;parser-compat-017/B/03&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;execution&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: {</span><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">&quot;status&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;succeeded&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;validity&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: {</span><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">&quot;status&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;valid&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;checks&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">    &quot;behavior&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;pass&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">    &quot;regression&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;pass&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">    &quot;integrity&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;fail&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">  },</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;accepted&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#569CD6">false</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#116329;--shiki-dark:#9CDCFE">  &quot;failure_code&quot;</span><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">: </span><span style="color:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#CE9178">&quot;protected_file_changed&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#D4D4D4">}</span></span>
</code></pre></div></div></div></div><div class="code-block-fade-overlay print:hidden" data-fade-overlay="true" aria-hidden="true" style="--fade-color-light:#ffffff;--fade-color-dark:#0B0C0E"></div></div>
<span data-as="p">Once the grader is written, the thing I most want to test is whether it accepts bad answers. Not changing the code at all, fixing only the ordinary inputs, deleting tests, and faking output can all serve as test samples. Add cases that use file links to bypass protected paths. A reference fix passing only verifies half of the grader’s job.</span>
<span data-as="p">Questions like “is this implementation overly complex” are hard to write as automatic assertions and can go to an LLM judge. Pin the model and rubric, and require conclusions to cite specific files or trajectory positions. For pairwise review, hide candidate identity, randomly swap the order, and check for misjudgments against human-labeled samples. Text the agent leaves behind is material to be judged; even if it says “ignore the previous grading rules,” it can never become an instruction to the judge.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="05-an-experiment-on-a-compaction-change"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#05-an-experiment-on-a-compaction-change" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">05 An experiment on a compaction change</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">With a run-and-verify pipeline in place, we can return to the opening question: does improving how a checkpoint preserves user constraints reduce constraint violations after compaction?</span>
<span data-as="p">MiniMax Code’s compaction already includes tool-output archiving, checkpoints, and programmatically appended recent state. This time only the way constraints are preserved changes; other prompts, archive thresholds, the model, and the budget stay the same. Change several things at once, and even if results improve, it’s hard to tell which change did it.</span>
<span data-as="p">I’d run this experiment at two levels. First, test the local mechanism: wait for tool calls to settle and background writes to stop, save a shared pre-compaction state, and let the baseline and candidate each continue from there. Observe whether user constraints survive, whether earlier evidence can be read back, whether work gets repeated afterward, and whether the task completes.</span>
<span data-as="p">That state can’t be just the messages. The workspace, session, compaction state, and archived artifacts referenced later all have to be restored; for tasks that depend on databases or external services, those resources have to be restored too. Archive files that are readable on the original machine but whose references break in a different directory will also skew the experiment. The public CLI can’t currently perform this fork in one step; it needs separate test interfaces for triggering compaction and restoring state.</span>
<span data-as="p">Then run end-to-end experiments from the task’s starting point, using the context capacity and trigger policy actually deployed. The candidate might trigger compaction earlier, or read files fewer times, or change the order of tool calls. All of these are effects of the full policy and should be counted.</span>
<span data-ramka-trigger="" class="zoom-image-trigger contents cursor-zoom-in"><span class="contents"><picture class="contents"><img data-path="images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg" src="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=0ed01328af492446b6b595f21990d785" alt="Evaluate compaction at two levels: a local mechanism test and an end-to-end evaluation" class="object-contain" width="1536" height="1024" decoding="async" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" srcSet="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=280&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=07cdee47c011a51a3f6bd3f4473d06a7 280w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=560&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=83b75916c1e96ebd0865aeed06d6fb1e 560w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=840&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=81e83097785c55a501702a3c92f6cc43 840w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=1100&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=0a65622988821a25be1ac2d53f28cfbe 1100w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=1650&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=649260a3a8ad51fa8a921cd788f74b9e 1650w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-compaction.jpg?w=2500&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=9c48bb26ff74e7b824d7a9b97d788483 2500w" data-optimize="true" style="aspect-ratio:1536 / 1024"/></picture></span></span><button type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-ramka-trigger="" aria-label="Expand image" class="inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-black/20 bg-white/55 text-neutral-900 shadow-[0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] backdrop-blur-[40px] backdrop-saturate-150 transition-[background-color,box-shadow,scale,opacity,color] duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-white/65 active:scale-[0.96] active:bg-white/75 focus:outline-hidden focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.25),0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-30 disabled:active:scale-100 dark:border-white/25 dark:bg-neutral-900/45 dark:text-white dark:shadow-[0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] dark:hover:bg-neutral-900/55 dark:active:bg-neutral-900/65 dark:focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(255_255_255/0.4),0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] absolute z-10 size-px overflow-hidden whitespace-nowrap [clip-path:inset(50%)] pointer-events-none focus-visible:size-9 focus-visible:overflow-visible focus-visible:[clip-path:none] focus-visible:pointer-events-auto in-[a]:hidden"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-4 shrink-0"><path d="M10.75 2.75H15.25V7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M15.25 2.75L10.75 7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 10.75V15.25H7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 15.25L7.25 10.75" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></button>
<span data-as="p"><em>Report the two kinds of experiments separately: local experiments compare behavior from the same starting point; end-to-end experiments observe the whole task.</em></span>
<span data-as="p">Some runs may never trigger compaction. They still belong to the end-to-end results and just need to be marked “not triggered.” Comparing only the tasks where compaction happened, after the fact, introduces selection bias: the candidate policy may itself change the trigger probability, so the two sides no longer hold the same set of cases. To explain the causal effect of the compaction step, go back to local experiments with trigger conditions specified in advance.</span>
<span data-as="p">Tasks also have to be designed so compaction actually matters. For example, a compatibility constraint raised early still has to be honored during later code changes; evidence from early tool output is needed later. Long tasks should cover multiple compactions. Simply stuffing in a large block of irrelevant text can test behavior under long inputs, but it can hardly tell you whether the agent forgets useful information.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="06-beyond-the-score-what-records-to-keep"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#06-beyond-the-score-what-records-to-keep" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">06 Beyond the score: what records to keep</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">When a task regresses, I want to be able to find what it read, which tools it called, and at which step it drifted from the requirements. When the grader changes, it should be possible to re-verify old artifacts. That’s the practical use of observability in evaluation.</span>
<span data-as="p">Records can be stored by purpose. The run manifest keeps versions and experimental conditions, the trajectory keeps model and tool events, and the artifact directory holds final files, environment snapshots, and verification output. Re-grading produces a new grade revision linked to the same frozen artifact, without overwriting old scores.</span>
<span data-as="p">A model name alone isn’t enough for the run manifest. You also need the model configuration and whatever service version is available, the Harness commit, fingerprints of the expanded prompt and tool schemas, and the task and grader versions. Permission policy, resources and budget, experiment group, and repetition index should also be queryable. If the server-side version can’t be pinned, record the run’s time window and carry that limitation into the analysis.</span>
<span data-as="p">MiniMax Code’s eval reporter already exposes collection interfaces for pre-call model snapshots, tool calls and results, usage, and turn end. Collection failures don’t interrupt the agent’s normal operation — a sensible trade-off for a product. In an evaluation, though, you also need evidence-completeness flags: a call missing from the logs may simply not have been captured.</span>
<span data-as="p">Usage can be missing too. <code>usageSource</code> and <code>usageIncomplete</code> in <code>ExecResult</code> exist for exactly this; in failure, compaction, and similar scenarios, the accounting may be incomplete. When computing cost, keep the unknown part unknown and record the confirmed cost as a lower bound. Filling gaps with zero underestimates cost; directly adding possibly overlapping cache, reasoning, and total-token fields may overcount.</span>
<span data-as="p">How failures are handled also needs to be decided up front. The table below is one workable convention:</span>
<!-- --><div role="presentation" style="position:relative;--scroll-area-corner-height:0px;--scroll-area-corner-width:0px" data-component-part="scroll-area" data-table-wrapper="true" class="min-w-0 [--page-padding:20px] flex w-[calc(100%+(var(--page-padding)*2))] my-[1em] py-[1em] -mx-(--page-padding) max-w-none [contain:inline-size]"><div role="region" data-id="base-ui-_R_tpbb9qnpfb39p7b9snpfiutb_-viewport" tabindex="-1" class="size-full rounded-[inherit] [--scroll-area-fade-size:32px] overflow-y-hidden! base-ui-disable-scrollbar" style="overflow:scroll" data-component-part="scroll-area-viewport" aria-label="Scrollable table"><div role="presentation" style="min-width:fit-content" data-component-part="scroll-area-content" class="flex"><div class="px-(--page-padding) grow max-w-none table"><table class="m-0 min-w-full w-full max-w-none table [&amp;_th]:text-left [&amp;_td[data-numeric]]:tabular-nums [&amp;_td]:min-w-[150px]"><thead><tr><th>Observed situation</th><th>How the result is treated</th><th>Retry?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Image pull fails before the environment starts</td><td>Scheduling infrastructure error; keep the record</td><td>Restart up to a preset limit</td></tr><tr><td>Agent exhausts its budget through looping or expensive operations</td><td>Task failure; counts in the pass-rate denominator</td><td>No re-running until it succeeds</td></tr><tr><td>Candidate hits the declared memory limit</td><td>Run failure under that resource constraint</td><td>Handle per task rules</td></tr><tr><td>The grader itself crashes</td><td>No valid grade yet; cannot count as a pass</td><td>Re-grade the same frozen artifact</td></tr><tr><td>Compaction events missing, but artifact verification complete</td><td>Task result can be kept; mechanism diagnostics unavailable</td><td>Flag the evidence gap</td></tr><tr><td>Agent actually asks a question that needs a human answer</td><td>Judge by the unattended or interactive task protocol</td><td>No ad-hoc human fallback</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div>
<span data-as="p">Budget exhaustion and machine failure can both look like “the process is gone”; telling them apart takes event and resource records. Each retry keeps the original trial, the reason, and a new attempt ID. Report the infrastructure error rate and task coverage alongside the score; otherwise dropping a few failures makes the numbers look good.</span>
<span data-as="p">If retries hit the limit with still no valid grade, report the gap and its impact on the conclusions. Model call failures can’t all be written off as external faults either: if this evaluation is meant to test the Harness’s recovery ability, they are part of the test. Which cases are excluded follows what was agreed before the experiment started.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="07-how-to-compare-two-versions"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#07-how-to-compare-two-versions" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">07 How to compare two versions</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">Groups A and B first align on tasks, model configuration, and budget, then run randomly interleaved within the same time window. Concurrency, rate limits, and cache warmth also need to match. If one group runs in the morning and the other at night, service fluctuations can get mixed into the version difference.</span>
<span data-as="p">Resource allocation itself also has to be controlled. Anthropic’s <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/infrastructure-noise" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="link">research on infrastructure noise</a> discusses how resource configuration affects agentic coding evals. So CPU, memory, network, and time limits should be recorded along with the model configuration.</span>
<span data-as="p">Decide how many times each task runs before starting. First compute each task’s pass rate in both groups, then compare the differences. Write the pass rates for task i as pᴀ,ᵢ and pʙ,ᵢ; with tasks weighted equally, the overall difference is Δ = (1/N) Σᵢ(pʙ,ᵢ − pᴀ,ᵢ). If you want to weight by the real usage distribution, fix the weights in advance. A task shouldn’t count more in the total just because it was run a few more times.</span>
<span data-ramka-trigger="" class="zoom-image-trigger contents cursor-zoom-in"><span class="contents"><picture class="contents"><img data-path="images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg" src="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=a0b73b6ab13f67273a2fbabb3e7eadcc" alt="Did the change actually help? Paired A/B tasks, aggregated task-level differences, and an independent holdout" class="object-contain" width="1536" height="1024" decoding="async" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" srcSet="https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=280&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=4ae83cf79fad8f04061e426d1c6b0209 280w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=560&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=7d5c3f7fa0a4dc1ce0e7714e965ed84f 560w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=840&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=67ddfd86d8261b0451d84ebf60a2fe9a 840w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=1100&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=d3f8e74b5269e51b8b11ee93b042b89a 1100w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=1650&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=9ee404f43f16c69bc138dee3b36ce476 1650w, https://mintcdn.com/agent-en/7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp/images/techblog/eval-ab.jpg?w=2500&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;n=7xgBTBZFmaXlhwRp&amp;q=85&amp;s=a8c9d06262238d66fb3a3fa761ac1039 2500w" data-optimize="true" style="aspect-ratio:1536 / 1024"/></picture></span></span><button type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-ramka-trigger="" aria-label="Expand image" class="inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-black/20 bg-white/55 text-neutral-900 shadow-[0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] backdrop-blur-[40px] backdrop-saturate-150 transition-[background-color,box-shadow,scale,opacity,color] duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-white/65 active:scale-[0.96] active:bg-white/75 focus:outline-hidden focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.25),0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.12),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.65)] disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-30 disabled:active:scale-100 dark:border-white/25 dark:bg-neutral-900/45 dark:text-white dark:shadow-[0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] dark:hover:bg-neutral-900/55 dark:active:bg-neutral-900/65 dark:focus-visible:shadow-[0_0_0_2px_rgb(255_255_255/0.4),0_0_0_0.5px_#000,0_0.5px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45),0_1px_2px_rgb(0_0_0/0.35),inset_0_0.5px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.14)] absolute z-10 size-px overflow-hidden whitespace-nowrap [clip-path:inset(50%)] pointer-events-none focus-visible:size-9 focus-visible:overflow-visible focus-visible:[clip-path:none] focus-visible:pointer-events-auto in-[a]:hidden"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-4 shrink-0"><path d="M10.75 2.75H15.25V7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M15.25 2.75L10.75 7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 10.75V15.25H7.25" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2.75 15.25L7.25 10.75" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></button>
<span data-as="p"><em>Compare each task first, then aggregate the differences and the uncertainty; finally verify the frozen candidate on an independent holdout set.</em></span>
<span data-as="p">Repeating the same task ten times shows how unstable runs are, but it still covers just one scenario. To estimate the gain on new tasks, use a paired cluster bootstrap by task, preserving the A/B relationship when resampling. If several tasks come from the same repo and are highly correlated, cluster by repo or task family. With few tasks the interval may not be reliable either, so list per-task results alongside it.</span>
<span data-as="p">I pay special attention to which failures the candidate newly introduces. A higher overall pass rate can mask a clear regression on one category of tasks. Integrity violations and human interventions get reported separately, and cost includes failed attempts. “Total cost of all attempts in the group / number of successful tasks” can represent cost per completed task; when there are zero successes or usage is incomplete, the estimate must be marked as not computable or incomplete.</span>
<span data-as="p">Latency reports must keep timeouts too. List the runtime of every attempt and the timeout rate, with latency of successful samples reported separately. A run killed at the deadline can’t count as finishing right at that second. If the product allows taking the best of several attempts, report the number of attempts together with the budget, and don’t compare it directly against single runs.</span>
<span data-as="p">The bar for accepting a candidate is best written down before the runs start: how much quality regression is tolerable, how much improvement is hoped for, how much cost can increase, and what hard requirements the integrity checks impose. The specific thresholds depend on product goals. Adjusting the bar after seeing scores makes it easy to rationalize a change you already wanted to merge. Even if no violations were observed this time, that only means none showed up in this batch of runs.</span>
<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="08-don’t-let-the-eval-set-turn-into-a-dev-set"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#08-don’t-let-the-eval-set-turn-into-a-dev-set" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-3 shrink-0"><path d="M8.50001 6.827C8.14801 6.995 7.81801 7.225 7.52701 7.517L7.51701 7.527C6.13601 8.908 6.13601 11.146 7.51701 12.527L9.69201 14.702C11.073 16.083 13.311 16.083 14.692 14.702L14.702 14.692C16.083 13.311 16.083 11.073 14.702 9.692L13.771 8.761" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path><path d="M9.50002 11.173C9.85202 11.005 10.182 10.775 10.473 10.483L10.483 10.473C11.864 9.092 11.864 6.854 10.483 5.473L8.30802 3.298C6.92702 1.917 4.68902 1.917 3.30802 3.298L3.29802 3.308C1.91702 4.689 1.91702 6.927 3.29802 8.308L4.22902 9.239" stroke="currentColor" width="1.5" linecap="round" linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">08 Don’t let the eval set turn into a dev set</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">Adding real failures to the regression set is useful. But if we keep tuning the Harness against the same set of tasks, we grow familiar with them, and rising scores don’t necessarily mean better performance on new tasks.</span>
<span data-as="p">The dev set is for reproducing and tuning, the validation set for choosing candidates, and the final holdout set is only used once a candidate is frozen. Split by repo, problem family, or time period, so the same task can’t appear on both sides under a different name. Once a holdout set has been looked at enough and used to change strategy, admit that it has become part of the dev set.</span>
<span data-as="p">Compaction experiments can have a targeted task set, but you should also run regressions on common tasks, check for side effects, and add new tasks regularly. Collecting only failure cases overweights hard scenarios; collecting only demo-friendly tasks makes the system look more reliable than it is.</span>
<span data-as="p">Nor does every PR need to run the whole evaluation. Run protocol, grader, and short regressions first; trigger the relevant behavioral evals when compaction, tools, or permissions change. Schedule expensive repeated experiments and new-task testing periodically within budget. <code>pnpm verify</code> in the source continues to check engineering correctness, while real-model task performance is evaluated separately, and the two are reported separately.</span>
<span data-as="p">If I were starting today, I’d begin with a single task: reset the environment, launch MiniMax Code, verify independently, and leave artifacts that can be re-examined. Then test the grader with a few known-good and known-bad versions. Only after all of that works would I add more tasks and concurrency. Building large-scale scheduling first will most likely just scale up the difficulty of debugging along with it.</span>
<span data-as="p">MiniMax Code’s public source already includes run entry points, structured events, and evaluation collection interfaces. If you want to get hands-on, you can hook up a Runner, or start by contributing a task that reliably reproduces a failure, or checking whether a grader wrongly accepts bad answers. All of this helps decide whether the next Harness change should stay.</span>
<span data-as="p">The project is on <a href="https://github.com/MiniMax-AI/minimax-code" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="link">GitHub</a>. Come discuss with reproductions and experiments in hand, and if you found this useful, a Star is always welcome.</span>
